Directives modify existing constructs. Template-head directives modify the containing
template. Source directives annotate the following declaration or statement when
their own target contract permits it. The compiler owns all directives.

### Template-head directives

A template-head directive belongs to the template that contains it:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    message #= [$md: Hello from Moth]
]

Template heads separate meaningful items with commas, including directives and
expressions. Each head directive has its own operand and compatibility rules.
See @../templates/#template-directives (Template directives).

### Source directives

Source directives attach to one following construct:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $export $doc [$md: Public greeting.]
    greeting #= "Hello"

    $doc [: Parses input text.]
    parse_text |source String| -> String:
        $todo [: Add Unicode escapes.]
        return source
    ;
]

`$export` publishes a declaration or strict direct-selection dependency clause
in `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth`. It cannot export an entry, ordinary runtime
statement, whole namespace or `$config` input. See
@../project-structure/#public-api (Public API) and
@../packages/#public-reexports (Public re-exports).

`$config` marks one explicitly typed top-level compile-time `#` binding in
`config.moth` or a selected source module as a build input:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

Its name is the input key, its type is the contract and its initializer is
the fallback. Only a marked input can omit its initializer. See
@../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build configuration values).

`$doc value` documents a declaration, including an eligible local declaration.
Its operand must evaluate at compile time to a `String`. The compiler attaches
the resolved text to the declaration's semantic identity as documentation
metadata. It does not publish a template fragment or emit runtime work.
`$doc` does not format its argument automatically. Use `$doc [$md: ...]`
for Markdown or `$doc [: ...]` for ordinary template content.

`$todo value` annotates a declaration or a complete executable statement,
including a branch, loop or return. It also requires a compile-time `String`
operand. It is validated and discarded for now, without a runtime effect,
builder output or automatic diagnostic. A future consumer can add a use without
changing its source syntax.

`$doc` and `$todo` are source directives, not template-head directives.
`$note` is the template-only comment directive. Neither source annotations nor
a discarded note contribute an entry page fragment.

### Attachment and stacking

A contiguous run of source directives attaches to the **immediately following**
declaration or statement. All directives in the run attach to that same
construct independently. Their order does not change the target or meaning.

Different directives may occupy separate consecutive lines or share a line,
separated by whitespace. Commas are not used:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $doc [: Returns a greeting.] $todo [: Consider localisation.]
    greeting || -> String:
        return "Hello"
    ;
]

The target construct begins on a later line, never after the final directive
on the same line. A comment-only line may intervene. A blank physical line,
scope boundary or end of file breaks attachment, and an intervening unrelated
construct cannot be skipped. LF and CRLF follow the same rules.

A directive consumes at most one permitted operand. Another source directive
terminates that operand only at its outer expression boundary. A nested template
directive, such as `$md` in `$doc [$md: text]`, remains part of that template.
No source directive can be the operand of another.

**Each directive name may occur at most once in one attachment run.** Every
duplicate is an error, even if the operands match or repetition would otherwise
be harmless. Diagnostics identify both authored occurrences. The same directive
may annotate a different following construct. There is no blanket pairwise
compatibility hierarchy: each distinct directive validates its own eligible
target and semantic requirements. Constraint directives therefore evaluate
the resulting program semantics independently of source order.

### Operands and diagnostics

The general directive surface is:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    $name
    $name value
    $name key = value
]

The directive contract selects which of these forms is legal. A named operand
has exactly one key and value, without a type annotation. Operands use the
ordinary compile-time expression rules and may contain balanced nested
templates. `$export` and `$config` accept no operand. Parenthesized calls
such as `$name(...)` are not directive syntax.

The compiler diagnoses unknown names, wrong locations, invalid operands,
nonconstant required values, duplicate names, missing targets and unsuitable
targets at their authored sites. It never searches past an invalid target to
find a later eligible one. A known directive in the wrong context is a
placement error, not an unknown name.

Module roles come from `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth`
filenames. Project and builder settings are ordinary records in
`config.moth`. Source files and builders cannot register directives or use
them as macros.

